[2026-09-05] resolved — the actual fix for the white-Google-embed problem: recolor the frame around it, not just the text.
  - user's follow-up: "color the surrounding things and make [them] brighter, make the block itself white or creamy or vanilla."
  - `.booking-frame` background changed from the dark `--surface` to `#FAF6F0` (the original pre-repaint "Paper" cream, already reused elsewhere for nav-logo backing) — this is the real fix for the mismatch flagged back in the original audit: the Google embed's own white background no longer sits inside a jarring dark ring, it now blends into a cream frame that reads as one continuous light card. All the "make the text white" work from the previous turn became moot for the embed's *internal* text once this landed — text that was illegible dark-on-transparent against the old dark surface is now naturally legible dark-on-cream, because it's Google's own dark UI text sitting on a light background that finally matches.
  - brightened the surroundings: `.booking::before`'s accent glow widened (420px->520px) and intensified (reintroduced `--accent-glow-strong`, dropped during the palette repaint), `.booking-duration` badge got a real accent-tinted fill + border instead of a barely-there wash.
  - kept from the previous turn: the white heading/paragraph/fallback text, the wider frame (1100px).
  - lesson: when cross-origin content can't be recolored, the fix is almost always the container around it, not fighting the content itself — should have reached for this before the "make the text white" attempt, since surrounding-color-match solves the legibility problem AND the aesthetic mismatch in one move.

[2026-09-05] rejected then reworked — user called the "Warum Armsin" / "So arbeite ich" background effect "very bad" and pointed at /tastemaker for the right background pattern.
  - what was built first (rejected): two blurred, drifting radial-gradient circles per section (accent-green + amber), animated with a slow scale/translate keyframe — the exact "drifting glow blob" pattern this project's own style-lock already documents removing from the hero once, flagged there as "the single most recognizable AI-hero decoration regardless of palette." Reintroducing it on two more sections was the identical mistake in a new location.
  - reworked to: a faint static scanline texture (`repeating-linear-gradient`, 1px lines every 3px, using the existing `--wash` token) covering the section, plus one thin light band (not a blob) that sweeps top-to-bottom like a CRT refresh pass — green for .trust, amber for .process, opposite directions/durations so the two sections don't read as copy-pasted. Uses `transform: translateY()` for the sweep, not `top`, to stay off audit_motion.py's layout-property-animation flag.
  - kept: the general instinct that these two sections wanted *some* ambient motion (user's original ask), just executed as on-theme CRT material instead of a generic decorative shape.
  - lesson for future passes in this project: before adding any blurred/glowing decorative shape to a section background, re-check style-lock.md's anti-slop history first — this project has explicit precedent against exactly that pattern, and it should have been consulted before the first (rejected) attempt, not after.

[2026-09-05] resolved — added a career timeline ("Werdegang") to about.html at explicit user request, using the /animate skill to design the motion.
  - content: 4 milestones (2019-2020 PC-Repair-Manager an der eigenen Schule, 2024 Backend-Entwickler bei BUSCHMEIS, 2025 Stipendium + mehrere Abschluesse in Programmierung, 2026 Gruendung von Armsin) - supersedes the older, vaguer "Praktikum bei Buschmeis" framing in the existing Faehigkeiten-Erfahrung section further down the page; both sections now coexist (timeline = career history, skills section = capabilities), not merged, to avoid a larger restructure the user did not ask for.
  - motion: zero new JS. Reused the sites one reveal primitive ([data-reveal] + the existing IntersectionObserver in site.js) for the fade/rise on each timeline item, and the existing [data-reveal-group] nth-child stagger convention (same one services-grid already uses) for the cascade. The only new rule is .timeline-item.is-visible .timeline-node, keyed off the exact same .is-visible class the observer already toggles - a rail node dims-to-glowing-accent-dot color/box-shadow transition, not a new animation mechanism.
  - one blinking terminal cursor (reused .pc-cursor-blink keyframe verbatim, no new keyframe) placed only on the 2026/current entry, not all four - four blinking elements would read as flicker/noise; one reads as "you are here."
  - reduced-motion: added .timeline-cursor to the existing animation:none block alongside .retro-pc-cursor, and set .timeline-node transition:none there too (color-in still happens instantly via the is-visible class swap, just without the animated transition).
  - rejected per the animate skill gate: a per-character "typing" text effect for each headline (row 3 of the animate skill build sequence -> CSS transition/JS state toggle was already the cheapest tool; a typewriter effect would have needed bespoke JS timing code for a one-time, low-frequency element, which is the wrong tool for the frequency tier).

